TZSET(3)                       P32W Unix                        TZSET(3)

NAME
       tzset, tzname - set the time zone from TZ

SYNOPSIS
       #include <time.h>

       void tzset(void);
       extern char *tzname[2];

DESCRIPTION
       The clock keeps UTC; local time is worked out by the library.
       tzset reads the time zone from the environment variable TZ
       or, if TZ is unset or empty, from the first line of the file
       /etc/TZ; with neither, local time is UTC. localtime(3),
       ctime(3) and mktime(3) call tzset the first time they are
       used; a program that changes TZ afterwards (setenv(3)) must
       call tzset itself.

       TZ is a POSIX rule (see tz(5)):

           std offset [dst [offset] [,start[/time],end[/time]]]

       std and dst are the names of standard and summer time:
       letters only, or anything between < and >, at most 15
       characters. offset is the time to add to local time to get
       UTC, [+|-]hh[:mm[:ss]]: positive west of Greenwich (PST8 is
       8 hours behind UTC). dst's offset defaults to an hour less
       than std's. start and end, when summer time begins and ends,
       are each Mm.w.d: the d-th day of the week (0 is Sunday) of
       week w (1 to 5; 5 means the last) of month m (1 to 12), at
       time (default 02:00), counted in standard time for start and
       in summer time for end. Without start and end, the United
       States rule applies (M3.2.0,M11.1.0). If start comes after
       end in the year, summer time runs over the new year (the
       southern hemisphere). A leading : is ignored.

       tzset sets tzname[0] to the name of standard time and
       tzname[1] to that of summer time (the same as tzname[0] when
       the zone has none); "UTC" without a zone.

EXAMPLES
       TZ=PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0               US Pacific
       TZ=CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3           central Europe
       TZ=JST-9                                Japan, no summer time
       TZ='<+0530>-5:30'                       India

       echo PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 > /etc/TZ   for every user

FILES
       /etc/TZ     the zone when TZ is not set

ENVIRONMENT
       TZ          the time zone rule

SEE ALSO
       date(1), time(3), mktime(3), getenv(3), tz(5)

BUGS
       There is no zoneinfo database: a TZ such as
       :America/Los_Angeles is not understood (it reads as a zone
       named America at UTC). Only the Mm.w.d form of start and end
       is understood; the Jn and n forms (day of the year) are not,
       and are silently replaced by the United States rule's dates.
       Nothing is checked: a month outside 1 to 12 gives nonsense.
       There are no timezone or daylight variables.
